# TokyoNight Storm with this setup's one deliberate deviation.
#
# Ghostty scans ~/.config/ghostty/themes before its own resources, so this file
# wins over the shipped "TokyoNight Storm". It is that file verbatim except for
# palette 1/9 and 6/14. Storm's red (#f7768e) leans pink, so both reds are this
# setup's clear red, relit in OKLab to L=0.78 (#ff7874) so it keeps the hue and
# still lands inside the palette's luminance band (APCA Lc 48.7 on #24283b,
# against Storm's own floor of 47.3). The unrelit #ff5858 measured Lc 41.3 and
# would have been the one dark outlier the switch to Storm was meant to remove.
#
# Cyan is Netresearch teal instead of Storm's #7dcfff, relit in OKLab to L=0.80
# so it survives a dark ground (Lc 65.4). Storm's cyan is a sky blue; every TUI
# that draws an accent reaches for ANSI 6, so leaving it blue meant herdr's own
# chrome and the plugins running inside it disagreed about what the accent is.
#
# Why Storm: measured across every dark theme Ghostty ships, Storm has the
# narrowest luminance band for the colours you actually read (Lc 47-72, span
# 24.5). The old nr-frappe spanned 40.9 and carried a Lc 38.8 accent, so the eye
# had to re-adapt on every glance. See herdr's [theme.custom] for the other half.
#
# This palette is what every program *inside* a pane resolves its colors
# against, including herdr's plugin TUIs. herdr's own chrome is separate and
# lives in ~/.config/herdr/config.toml under [theme.custom]; the two have to be
# kept in agreement by hand, because herdr never reads this file.
palette = 0=#1d202f
palette = 1=#ff7874
palette = 2=#9ece6a
palette = 3=#e0af68
palette = 4=#7aa2f7
palette = 5=#bb9af7
palette = 6=#6ccfdb
palette = 7=#a9b1d6
palette = 8=#4e5575
palette = 9=#ff7874
palette = 10=#9ece6a
palette = 11=#e0af68
palette = 12=#7aa2f7
palette = 13=#bb9af7
palette = 14=#6ccfdb
palette = 15=#c0caf5
background = #24283b
foreground = #c0caf5
cursor-color = #c0caf5
cursor-text = #1d202f
selection-background = #364a82
selection-foreground = #c0caf5
